@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix probe: <http://example.org/orexis/moisture-probe#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/moisture-probe#> .
@prefix wokwi: <http://example.org/orexis/wokwi#> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix water: <http://example.org/orexis/water#> .
@prefix mc: <http://example.org/orexis/microcontroller#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/moisture-probe> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Capacitive soil moisture probe" ;
    rdfs:comment """One part, its own package. Three legs — an analog output, power and ground — and two calibration numbers that belong to the individual probe rather than to the model.""" .

:CapacitiveMoistureProbe a owl:Class ;
    mc:modelName "Capacitive Soil Moisture Sensor v2.0" ; rdfs:subClassOf mc:Peripheral ;
    # A CUSTOM CHIP rather than a catalogue part — the `chip-` prefix is the tell — so a Wokwi
    # project referencing it must also carry soil-moisture-sensor.chip.json and .chip.c. That is
    # a real constraint on anyone opening the generated diagram and it is said in the README
    # beside it, because a missing chip fails at load with nothing pointing at the cause.
    #
    # It was drawn as a potentiometer for a while, on my claim that Wokwi had no soil probe.
    # Wrong, and wrong in the way worth noticing: a plausible stand-in is harder to doubt than
    # a missing one, so it would have simulated perfectly well while misdescribing the bench.
    wokwi:part "chip-soil-moisture-sensor" ;
    wokwi:pin [ mc:pinRole mc:AnalogInPinRole ; wokwi:name "SIG" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:PowerPinRole    ; wokwi:name "VCC" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:GroundPinRole   ; wokwi:name "GND" ] ;
    rdfs:label "Capacitive soil moisture probe" ;
    rdfs:comment """Reports a raw ADC count that falls as the soil wets. A class rather than a model string because how you READ it is what differs; the model is for the person holding it.

Deliberately NOT a subclass of sensing:Sensor, though every probe in this repository is one. The two typings are orthogonal and that is what makes them useful: sensing:Sensor says what a thing is TO THE SOCIETY — polled by an agent, with a subject, an observed property and a reading topic — while this says what it physically IS. The same node in world.ttl carries both, from two directions.

Welding them would break the case the whole simulation rests on. A simulated sensor is an sensing:Sensor with no peripheral class and no pins at all, and an agent must not be able to tell it from hardware; if being a probe implied being a sensor, the class would stop describing the part and start describing its employment. It would also make a spare probe lying on the bench — real, wired to nothing, polled by nobody — unstateable.""" .

#################  What it detects, and what that stands in for (#100)  #################
#
#  SSN's words for the two ends of the conversion this package's calibration numbers serve:
#  the probe DETECTS capacitance (ssn:Stimulus — what it physically responds to), and
#  capacitance IS A PROXY FOR soil moisture (what the society actually wants known). The
#  raw-versus-quantity split that bytes-become-a-quantity-in-stages drew in prose, in terms.
#
#  The proxy statement couples this part to the water domain, honestly: a part BUILT to sense
#  moisture names moisture, the same way market's bidding beliefs carry the one deliberately
#  domain-coupled literal. What drifts when a probe ages is exactly this relationship — the
#  capacitance stops standing in for the moisture as well as it did — which is why a
#  calibration goes stale while neither the property nor the electronics changed.
#
#  `ssn:detects` reaches every device through the class, by the closure's hasValue rule: a
#  datasheet fact, stated once on the part, entailed onto each probe built from it.

:capacitance a ssn:Stimulus ; rdfs:label "capacitance of the soil" ;
    ssn:isProxyFor water:SoilMoisture .

:CapacitiveMoistureProbe rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty ssn:detects ; owl:hasValue :capacitance ] .

:rawDry a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "raw when dry" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this probe reads in air — a value OF THE STIMULUS (#100): a capacitance count, not a moisture. A fact about THIS probe in THIS pot — two identical probes differ — so it belongs to the instance, not to the firmware, which is per model. The scaling family's TwoPoint member consumes exactly this pair, which is why its parameters are stimulus-side by construction." .
:rawWet a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "raw when wet" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it reads in saturated soil — the stimulus end again, never the property. Not free water: water is wetter than soil ever gets, so calibrating against it makes the top of the scale unreachable." .
